ADS-007 — Canonical Communication Envelope (CCE)

Document ID: ADS-007
Title: Canonical Communication Envelope (CCE)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Communication Envelope (CCE), the standard transport wrapper for all Atlas communications.

The CCE enables secure, traceable, interoperable, and technology-independent exchange of Canonical Atlas Objects between services, agents, workflows, organizations, and external systems.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Communication envelope model
Envelope structure
Payload abstraction
Routing metadata
Correlation model
Security metadata
Delivery metadata
Communication governance

The CCE applies to every message exchanged within or across Atlas environments.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Communication Envelope (CCE) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that encapsulates a payload together with standardized metadata required for routing, security, governance, traceability, delivery, and interoperability.

The envelope carries the payload.

The payload remains semantically independent of the envelope.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Communication Envelope SHALL:

Standardize communication.
Preserve payload integrity.
Preserve semantic independence.
Enable secure transport.
Enable interoperability.
Enable routing.
Enable monitoring.
Enable auditing.
5. Inheritance

CCE inherits all mandatory properties of the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
Communication Envelope
        │
 ┌──────┼────────────┐
 ▼      ▼            ▼
Internal External   Human
Envelope Envelope   Envelope

Future communication profiles SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Payload Model

The payload MAY contain any Canonical Atlas Object.

Examples include:

Canonical Engineering Artifact (CEA)
Decision Record (DR)
Canonical Event (CEV)
Interface Contract (CIC)
Trust Object (CTO)
Workflow Object (CWO)
Policy Object (CPO)

The envelope SHALL NOT alter payload semantics.

7. Mandatory Envelope Properties

Every CCE SHALL define:

Property	Description
Envelope ID	Globally unique identifier
Envelope Type	Communication classification
Payload Type	Canonical object type
Payload Reference	Object or embedded payload
Sender	Originating participant
Receiver	Intended participant(s)
Timestamp	Creation time
Correlation ID	Related activity
Workflow ID	Related workflow
Message Priority	Delivery priority
Delivery Guarantee	Delivery expectation
8. Routing Model

The envelope SHALL support routing metadata including:

Sender
Receiver
Destination
Communication Domain
Routing Path
Message Priority
Delivery Channel

Routing information SHALL NOT modify payload content.

9. Correlation Model

Every envelope SHALL support:

Correlation ID
Causation ID
Conversation ID
Workflow ID
Project ID
Transaction ID

These identifiers enable end-to-end traceability.

10. Delivery Model

Atlas supports the following delivery guarantees.

Guarantee	Description
Best Effort	Delivery attempted without confirmation
At Most Once	No duplicates, possible loss
At Least Once	Guaranteed delivery, duplicates possible
Exactly Once	Guaranteed single delivery

The applicable guarantee SHALL be declared.

11. Security Model

Every CCE SHALL include security metadata.

Required fields include:

Security Classification
Authentication Context
Authorization Context
Encryption Status
Integrity Verification
Trust References

The payload MAY be encrypted independently of the envelope.

12. Communication Lifecycle

Every CCE SHALL support the following lifecycle.

Created
   │
Validated
   │
Dispatched
   │
Transmitted
   │
Received
   │
Acknowledged
   │
Processed
   │
Archived

Lifecycle transitions SHALL be auditable.

13. Provenance

Every envelope SHALL preserve:

Sender identity
Communication path
Delivery history
Processing history
Receiving participant
Security context

Communication provenance SHALL be immutable.

14. Quality Requirements

Every CCE SHALL satisfy:

Reliability
Integrity
Confidentiality
Traceability
Availability
Interoperability
Delivery assurance
Auditability
15. Governance

Every envelope SHALL define:

Owner
Communication policy
Retention policy
Delivery policy
Security policy
Audit policy

Governance SHALL accompany every communication.

16. Conformance

A Canonical Communication Envelope conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Preserves payload semantics.
Implements mandatory communication metadata.
Supports routing.
Supports security metadata.
Supports delivery guarantees.
Maintains provenance.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Federated communication profiles.
Cross-cloud communication.
Autonomous agent communication.
Digital Twin synchronization channels.
Semantic routing engines.
Adaptive communication optimization.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical communication semantics.

